          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8.9 A Deeper Look into Atomic Structure"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.12,
            0.381,
            0.14
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8.9.1 Isotopes"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.142,
            0.89,
            0.246
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Dalton proposed that all atoms of an element are identical and have the same mass. But scientists later discovered that there were atoms of the same element that could have the same number of protons (atomic number, Z) yet could have different numbers of neutrons, and thus, different mass numbers "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( A = p ^ { + } + n ^ { 0 } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . These ʻtwin atomsʼ with the same atomic number but different mass numbers are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "isotopes",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.248,
            0.89,
            0.354
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us take hydrogen as an example. Naturally occurring hydrogen is a mixture of three different isotopes: "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "^ 1 _ { 1 } \\mathrm { H }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (protium, ~99.98%), "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { \\phantom { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } } } { 1 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " H (deuterium, ~0.015%), and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "^ 3 _ { 1 } \\mathrm { H }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (tritium, in traces) (Fig. 8.12). All of these contain one proton each, whereas deuterium contains one neutron, and tritium contains two neutrons. Can you guess how many electrons each of these isotopes have?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
